WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.820
Who could have imagined that a man who&nbsp;
spent his days feeding pigeons in a park&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.740
near the New York Public Library was one&nbsp;
of the most brilliant minds in the world.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.840
Nikola Tesla was so smart that he&nbsp;
could picture all his ideas and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.980
the designs for them in his head.&nbsp;
He didn’t need models or drawings.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.800
But although the Serbian-American was a&nbsp;
famous inventor, he died penniless after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.920
his most ambitious project came crashing down.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.940
How dramatic a turn his life took.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:37.380
At one point, he could have easily&nbsp;
been the richest person on the planet.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:43.440
Royalty payments on his alternating current&nbsp;
power system were worth an absolute fortune,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.920
hundreds of millions of&nbsp;
dollars adjusted for inflation.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.600
Today, our world is still&nbsp;
predominantly powered by AC in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.860
which the flow of electricity changes&nbsp;
direction periodically. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.520
it suffers less energy loss when&nbsp;
transmitted over long distances.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.800
On the other hand, Thomas Edison’s&nbsp;
direct current - DC - travels in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.400
only one direction and is tough&nbsp;
to transmit over long distances.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.740
AC is the type of electric current&nbsp;
generated by most power plants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.500
including Tesla’s plant at Niagara Falls.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.880
Niagara Falls started the&nbsp;
electrification of the world.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.160
Tesla and American industrialist George&nbsp;
Westinghouse built the world’s first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:32.580
hydroelectric power plant here in 1895,&nbsp;
using AC to harness the power of the falls.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:37.740
After such a great success, Tesla&nbsp;
could have retired very comfortably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:45.000
but instead, he was determined to outdo himself&nbsp;
by achieving a new form…of global communication.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.220
Trouble is…he had no money to turn&nbsp;
his latest dream into reality.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:55.800
How can that be? Considering his&nbsp;
AC system made him very wealthy.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:01.500
Westinghouse purchased Tesla’s AC&nbsp;
patents for $60,000 in the form of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:07.320
$5,000 cash and 150 shares in Westinghouse&nbsp;
Electric and Manufacturing Company.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.800
Westinghouse also agreed to pay Tesla royalties&nbsp;
based on how much electricity he could sell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.900
amounting to $2.50 per horsepower&nbsp;
of electrical capacity sold.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:24.560
Tesla should have been rich. But he&nbsp;
wasn’t. Because…of his kind heart.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.640
But Westinghouse's company suffered&nbsp;
greatly as it battled Edison’s company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:33.720
in which Edison tried to show&nbsp;
how dangerous Tesla’s AC was.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.144
Westinghouse was nearly bankrupt

00:02:36.194 --> 00:02:41.474
and industrialist J.P. Morgan intended to&nbsp;
starve out Westinghouse’s company and buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.474 --> 00:02:44.954
up Tesla’s patents in an effort&nbsp;
to dominate the energy market.

00:02:45.674 --> 00:02:49.514
Westinghouse begged Tesla for&nbsp;
relief from having to pay the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.514 --> 00:02:52.094
promised royalties to try to save his company.

00:02:53.174 --> 00:02:58.454
In response, in what can only be described&nbsp;
as a gesture of pure selflessness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.454 --> 00:03:04.034
Tesla tore up his contract…he was&nbsp;
so moved by his friend’s pleas.

00:03:04.034 --> 00:03:09.314
Nikola Tesla single-handedly&nbsp;
saved the Westinghouse company.

00:03:10.094 --> 00:03:15.614
He was grateful to George Westinghouse, his friend&nbsp;
who believed in him, who never swindled him.

00:03:15.614 --> 00:03:19.814
And besides, Tesla had even&nbsp;
bigger ideas to pursue.

00:03:20.414 --> 00:03:24.314
Yet walking away from the royalties&nbsp;
would come back to haunt him.

00:03:24.314 --> 00:03:29.834
Because it left him struggling financially…to&nbsp;
the point where it dashed his ultimate dream.

00:03:30.734 --> 00:03:32.714
After the success of Niagara Falls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.714 --> 00:03:37.514
Tesla returned to his lab. There was&nbsp;
nothing he loved more than experimenting.

00:03:37.514 --> 00:03:41.174
Even the fire that gutted his&nbsp;
New York laboratory couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.174 --> 00:03:43.874
stop him. We don’t know why it caught fire.

00:03:44.654 --> 00:03:50.234
In 1891, he had invented the remarkable&nbsp;
Tesla coil, which produced high-voltage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.234 --> 00:03:53.054
high-frequency alternating current electricity.

00:03:53.054 --> 00:03:56.354
With high frequencies, he pioneered the use of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.354 --> 00:04:01.514
x-rays for medical purposes. This is&nbsp;
an x-ray Tesla took of his own hand.

00:04:01.514 --> 00:04:07.454
But that paled in comparison to the time&nbsp;
he waved vacuum tubes around like a Jedi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.454 --> 00:04:11.954
illuminating them wirelessly after having&nbsp;
transmitted energy through the air!

00:04:12.494 --> 00:04:16.874
The wireless transmission of energy&nbsp;
became his lifelong obsession.

00:04:17.534 --> 00:04:23.414
He imagined a wireless globe - transmitting&nbsp;
free electricity across the Atlantic with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.414 --> 00:04:27.914
no wires…and took his idea to the&nbsp;
wealthy American banker J.P. Morgan.

00:04:27.914 --> 00:04:32.534
Morgan put up $150,000 to&nbsp;
build a giant transmission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.534 --> 00:04:35.894
tower at Wardenclyffe on Long Island, New York.

00:04:35.894 --> 00:04:39.494
Tesla imagined that it would&nbsp;
be the first of a network of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.494 --> 00:04:44.294
towers worldwide. He believed that these&nbsp;
towers would allow him to send electricity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.294 --> 00:04:48.794
through the atmosphere which anyone with&nbsp;
the correct equipment could then tap into.

00:04:49.394 --> 00:04:54.254
This image is an artistic impression&nbsp;
kindly supplied to me by Alan Bellows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.254 --> 00:04:58.754
of Damn Interesting; the real tower&nbsp;
never crackled with electricity.

00:04:59.294 --> 00:05:04.754
Tesla explained at the time: “…it will be&nbsp;
possible for a businessman in New York to dictate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.754 --> 00:05:10.094
instructions, and have them instantly appear&nbsp;
in type at his office in London or elsewhere”.

00:05:10.754 --> 00:05:15.974
He was well ahead of his time. It&nbsp;
was incredibly prescient of him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.974 --> 00:05:21.494
imagine a global wireless communication system&nbsp;
similar to what the internet offers us today.

00:05:22.214 --> 00:05:28.394
But not long after construction began in&nbsp;
1901, Tesla started to run out of money.

00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:33.134
He appealed to J.P. Morgan for&nbsp;
more financing without any luck.

00:05:33.134 --> 00:05:38.354
Investors were instead throwing their&nbsp;
support behind a young Italian inventor.

00:05:39.674 --> 00:05:46.634
In 1901, Guglielmo Marconi successfully sent a&nbsp;
radio signal from England to Newfoundland, Canada.

00:05:46.634 --> 00:05:49.814
He used Tesla’s radio patents to do it.

00:05:49.814 --> 00:05:55.574
Tesla realized his coils could transmit&nbsp;
and receive powerful radio signals and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.574 --> 00:05:59.774
applied for radio patents which the&nbsp;
U.S. Patent Office granted in 1900.

00:05:59.774 --> 00:06:04.754
So Marconi’s feat didn’t bother Tesla,&nbsp;
who remarked to one of his engineers:

00:06:04.754 --> 00:06:10.034
"Marconi is a good fellow. Let him continue.&nbsp;
He is using seventeen of my patents."

00:06:10.034 --> 00:06:15.074
Marconi tried to file radio patents&nbsp;
himself in the U.S., but the Patent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.074 --> 00:06:20.354
Office turned down repeated applications as&nbsp;
it argued they were too similar to Tesla’s.

00:06:20.354 --> 00:06:24.614
However, as Tesla found out the&nbsp;
hard way, things can change.

00:06:24.614 --> 00:06:31.154
In 1904, the Patent Office reversed&nbsp;
its earlier decisions and awarded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.154 --> 00:06:34.154
Marconi a patent for the invention of the radio.

00:06:35.054 --> 00:06:38.414
The reason behind the reversal&nbsp;
has never been explained.

00:06:39.434 --> 00:06:43.454
Some suspect the powerful backing&nbsp;
Marconi received in the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.454 --> 00:06:46.934
including that by Edison, played a role.

00:06:46.934 --> 00:06:51.434
Marconi was credited as the inventor&nbsp;
of the radio…and became rich.

00:06:51.434 --> 00:06:55.274
He even won the Nobel Prize&nbsp;
in Physics as a result!

00:06:55.274 --> 00:06:59.834
Tesla was furious and sued the Marconi Company for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.834 --> 00:07:04.754
patent infringement yet couldn’t afford to&nbsp;
litigate a case against a big corporation.

00:07:04.754 --> 00:07:10.514
He also couldn’t find anyone interested in&nbsp;
investing more money in his tower of dreams.

00:07:11.114 --> 00:07:16.574
In 1917, Tesla’s tower was&nbsp;
dismantled to pay off his creditors.

00:07:17.294 --> 00:07:22.034
Tesla was in debt to the heirs of the Waldorf&nbsp;
Astoria Hotel where he had been staying.

00:07:22.034 --> 00:07:27.014
When his friend and wealthy investor&nbsp;
John Jacob Astor died on the Titanic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.014 --> 00:07:30.254
Astor’s heirs demanded that Tesla pay his debt. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:30.254 --> 00:07:33.974
He did so by demolishing his tower&nbsp;
and selling the scrap metal. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:34.694 --> 00:07:38.954
When his greatest dream was&nbsp;
out of reach, Tesla lamented:

00:07:38.954 --> 00:07:44.474
”It is not a dream. It is a simple feat&nbsp;
of scientific electrical engineering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.474 --> 00:07:49.394
only expensive—blind,&nbsp;
faint-hearted, doubting world!”

00:07:50.594 --> 00:07:53.714
Tesla began to withdraw from that world.

00:07:54.794 --> 00:07:58.394
He showed signs of obsessive-compulsive disorder.

00:07:58.394 --> 00:08:01.754
He was obsessed with the number 3 and would walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.754 --> 00:08:04.454
around a block three times&nbsp;
before entering a building.

00:08:04.454 --> 00:08:07.634
He’d count his steps wherever he walked.

00:08:07.634 --> 00:08:12.854
He compulsively counted the cubic content&nbsp;
of bowls of soup or cups of coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.854 --> 00:08:15.254
otherwise, his meals were not enjoyable.

00:08:16.034 --> 00:08:19.694
Near the end of his life, he&nbsp;
became obsessed with pigeons.

00:08:19.694 --> 00:08:22.454
Especially a white female pigeon.

00:08:22.454 --> 00:08:24.254
He is believed to have said:

00:08:24.254 --> 00:08:28.154
“…I loved that pigeon, I loved&nbsp;
her as a man loves a woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.154 --> 00:08:33.854
and she loved me...As long as I had&nbsp;
her, there was a purpose in my life.”

00:08:34.514 --> 00:08:36.014
Tesla never married.

00:08:36.554 --> 00:08:43.454
The pigeon apparently died in his arms, and, at&nbsp;
that point, he knew his life’s work was finished.

00:08:44.654 --> 00:08:49.274
Nikola Tesla would live out the last ten&nbsp;
years of his life at the New Yorker Hotel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.274 --> 00:08:54.914
paid for by the Westinghouse Corporation&nbsp;
which also gave him a “consulting fee” of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.914 --> 00:09:00.494
$125 a month. This is the company that&nbsp;
was in debt to him for having given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.494 --> 00:09:05.714
up royalties worth millions of dollars for&nbsp;
the sake of his friend George Westinghouse.

00:09:06.734 --> 00:09:10.094
He began to fade away into history, one of the few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.094 --> 00:09:14.774
exceptions being the time he turned 75&nbsp;
and made the cover of Time Magazine.

00:09:15.614 --> 00:09:23.474
Tesla died in his hotel room alone on&nbsp;
January 7, 1943…without a penny to his name.

00:09:23.474 --> 00:09:25.634
He was 86 years old.

00:09:26.594 --> 00:09:29.354
A few months after his death, the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.354 --> 00:09:33.794
Supreme Court overturned Marconi’s&nbsp;
radio patent in favor of Tesla’s.

00:09:34.334 --> 00:09:38.774
But the Court had a selfish reason&nbsp;
for doing so; the Marconi Company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.774 --> 00:09:42.554
was suing the U.S. government for&nbsp;
use of its patents in World War I.

00:09:42.554 --> 00:09:47.594
So, the Court avoided the lawsuit&nbsp;
by simply restoring Tesla’s patent.

00:09:48.254 --> 00:09:52.334
When I worked on an earlier video&nbsp;
about Nikola Tesla some pointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.334 --> 00:09:56.174
out that The Forgotten Genius&nbsp;
was not an apt thumbnail title.

00:09:56.174 --> 00:10:01.514
Personally, I grew up in Canada learning&nbsp;
much more about Thomas Edison, an American,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.514 --> 00:10:05.894
than Nikola Tesla, an immigrant who&nbsp;
was born in present-day Croatia.

00:10:06.494 --> 00:10:11.054
But perhaps viewers have a point,&nbsp;
and he really isn’t forgotten.

00:10:11.054 --> 00:10:14.894
Today, Tesla is once again capturing the public’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.894 --> 00:10:18.614
imagination. Because an electric&nbsp;
car company bears his name.

00:10:19.274 --> 00:10:25.394
Elon Musk’s company paid homage&nbsp;
to Nikola Tesla with a dazzling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.394 --> 00:10:29.774
display of drones earlier this year that&nbsp;
formed a visage of the brilliant engineer.

